1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,243
In June 1816, it snowed in New England.

2
00:00:04,567 --> 00:00:05,782
Not a light frost.

3
00:00:05,832 --> 00:00:10,983
Snow — in the first week of summer — on
fields that had just been planted.

4
00:00:11,300 --> 00:00:15,964
In Europe, it rained for months.
The harvest rotted.

5
00:00:16,014 --> 00:00:19,046
People ate moss, nettles and cats.

6
00:00:19,367 --> 00:00:27,548
And in a villa on Lake Geneva, an
eighteen-year-old stuck indoors by the storms

7
00:00:27,598 --> 00:00:30,569
started writing Frankenstein.

8
00:00:30,900 --> 00:00:32,067
Nobody alive knew why.

9
00:00:32,117 --> 00:00:36,602
The cause was on the other side of the world —
and it had happened a year earlier.

10
00:00:36,933 --> 00:00:41,945
This is the story of the volcano that
cancelled summer for the whole planet.

11
00:00:42,267 --> 00:00:45,903
In April 1815, on the Indonesian
island of Sumbawa,

12
00:00:45,953 --> 00:00:48,361
a mountain called Tambora woke up.

13
00:00:48,700 --> 00:00:52,664
The explosions were heard more than two
thousand kilometres away.

14
00:00:52,714 --> 00:00:57,790
Soldiers on other islands thought it was cannon
fire, and went looking for pirates.

15
00:00:58,133 --> 00:01:00,552
It was the biggest eruption in
recorded history.

16
00:01:00,602 --> 00:01:03,330
The mountain lost more than a
kilometre of its height.

17
00:01:03,667 --> 00:01:07,596
Around ten thousand people died in
the eruption itself.

18
00:01:07,646 --> 00:01:13,239
Tens of thousands more died in the famine that
followed on the islands nearby.

19
00:01:13,567 --> 00:01:17,171
But the ash that fell on Sumbawa
wasn't the dangerous part.

20
00:01:17,500 --> 00:01:19,356
The dangerous part went up.

21
00:01:19,700 --> 00:01:24,904
Tambora blew around sixty million tonnes of
sulphur gas into the stratosphere —

22
00:01:24,954 --> 00:01:27,298
twenty, thirty, forty kilometres up.

23
00:01:27,633 --> 00:01:29,920
Up there, there's no rain to wash it out.

24
00:01:29,970 --> 00:01:32,427
The gas turned into a haze of
tiny droplets,

25
00:01:32,477 --> 00:01:35,220
and the winds spread it around
the entire planet.

26
00:01:35,567 --> 00:01:41,313
A veil, too thin to see — but thick enough to
bounce sunlight back into space.

27
00:01:41,633 --> 00:01:44,999
The whole Northern Hemisphere cooled
by around half a degree.

28
00:01:45,049 --> 00:01:46,399
That sounds like nothing.

29
00:01:46,733 --> 00:01:50,114
For farmers, it was everything.

30
00:01:50,433 --> 00:01:54,184
In New England, there was frost in
every month of 1816.

31
00:01:54,234 --> 00:01:58,675
Corn froze in the fields in June, again
in July, again in August.

32
00:01:59,000 --> 00:02:04,250
Americans gave the year a nickname:
eighteen hundred and froze to death.

33
00:02:04,567 --> 00:02:09,254
Thousands of families gave up on New
England altogether and moved west,

34
00:02:09,304 --> 00:02:10,522
to Ohio and beyond.

35
00:02:10,867 --> 00:02:14,707
In Europe it was worse. The
price of wheat doubled.

36
00:02:14,757 --> 00:02:20,505
There were food riots in Britain and France.
In Switzerland, people starved.

37
00:02:20,833 --> 00:02:24,106
In China's Yunnan province, the
rice harvest failed.

38
00:02:24,156 --> 00:02:26,215
In India, the monsoon went wrong.

39
00:02:26,533 --> 00:02:29,583
Even horses went hungry —
there were no oats.

40
00:02:29,633 --> 00:02:32,889
Some historians think that's
why, the next year,

41
00:02:32,939 --> 00:02:38,055
a German inventor built a horse you didn't
have to feed: the first bicycle.

42
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:43,276
Back on Lake Geneva, the storms kept a group of
young English writers indoors for

43
00:02:43,326 --> 00:02:43,580
days.

44
00:02:43,900 --> 00:02:49,257
Lord Byron, bored, challenged everyone
to write a ghost story.

45
00:02:49,600 --> 00:02:52,041
Mary Shelley came up with Frankenstein.

46
00:02:52,091 --> 00:02:57,278
Byron's doctor, John Polidori, wrote The Vampyre
— the first modern vampire story.

47
00:02:57,600 --> 00:03:02,112
Byron himself wrote a poem called Darkness —
about the day the Sun goes out.

48
00:03:02,433 --> 00:03:07,229
One cold summer gave us two of the most
famous monsters in fiction.

49
00:03:07,567 --> 00:03:10,971
And everywhere the veil went, the
sunsets turned strange.

50
00:03:11,300 --> 00:03:15,997
For years afterwards, the skies over Europe
glowed blood red and orange.

51
00:03:16,333 --> 00:03:20,274
Scientists have measured the colours in
paintings from those years.

52
00:03:20,324 --> 00:03:23,967
After big eruptions, the painted
sunsets really do get redder.

53
00:03:24,300 --> 00:03:28,663
At the time, nobody connected any of this to a
volcano on the other side of the

54
00:03:28,713 --> 00:03:28,998
world.

55
00:03:29,333 --> 00:03:34,003
It took more than a century — until an American
physicist named William Humphreys

56
00:03:34,053 --> 00:03:35,926
put the numbers together in 1920.

57
00:03:36,267 --> 00:03:38,941
In 1991, we got to watch it happen again.

58
00:03:38,991 --> 00:03:44,255
Mount Pinatubo in the Philippines cooled the
whole planet by about half a degree

59
00:03:44,305 --> 00:03:45,185
for two years.

60
00:03:45,500 --> 00:03:49,776
Tambora threw out more than ten times
as much rock as Pinatubo.

61
00:03:50,100 --> 00:03:55,311
Volcanologists put the chance of another
eruption that size this century at about

62
00:03:55,361 --> 00:03:56,025
one in six.

63
00:03:56,367 --> 00:03:59,401
One in six. The same odds as
rolling a die.

64
00:03:59,733 --> 00:04:02,657
This time, at least, we'd know why
the summer didn't come.
